\fs96 \cf2 Pixi basics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Pixi is a CLI tool {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prefix.dev/blog/launching_pixi"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 from Prefix.dev}} that we recommend you use to manage your package dependencies and virtual environments when developing with the Modular Platform and Mojo language.\
We like Pixi so much, we created a fork called Magic, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://forum.modular.com/t/migrating-from-magic-to-pixi/1530"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Magic is now deprecated}} because Pixi can do everything we need. So we created this page to help you get started with Pixi. For more details, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 the official Pixi docs}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 All our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/modular/modular/tree/modular/v26.2/max/examples"}}{\fldrslt \ul GitHub code examples}} include a 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb13 pixi.toml
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  file. This file configures the environment to make sure we all use the same packages and get the same results\'97you just need to install 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb13 pixi
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Install Pixi\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can install Pixi with this command:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 curl\cf16  \cf17 -fsSL\cf16  https://pixi.sh/install.sh \cf5 |\cf16  \cf14 bash\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Then enable auto-completion:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
bash\cf6 zshfish\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 eval\cf16  \cf20 "\cf17 $(pixi completion --shell bash)\cf20 "\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you're using a different terminal, see more options in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/installation/#autocompletion"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 the Pixi docs}}.\
You can also update 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 pixi
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  with this command:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi self-update\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For version information, see the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/CHANGELOG/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Pixi changelog}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb23 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb23 If you've used a package manager like brew, mamba, conda, paru etc. to install pixi you must use the built-in update mechanism. For example:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf16 \cb13 brew upgrade pixi\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Create a project and virtual environment\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can create a project with its own packages and virtual environment using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/init/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi init}}.\
Normally, you must use the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/init/#arg---channel"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 --channel}} argument to specify where to get packages. Instead, we recommend you set default channels in your {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/dev/reference/pixi_configuration/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 user config file}} (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 $HOME/.pixi/config.toml
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
For example, here's how to add the Modular and conda-forge channels as defaults:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 mkdir\cf16  \cf17 -p\cf16  \cf14 $HOME\cf16 /.pixi\cb1 \
\cf14 \cb15 echo\cf16  \cf20 'default-channels = ["https://conda.modular.com/max-nightly", "conda-forge"]'\cf16  \cf5 \\\cf16 \cb1 \
\cb15   \cf5 >>\cf16  \cf14 $HOME\cf16 /.pixi/config.toml\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now those channels are always included in your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 pixi.toml
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when you create a project:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi init example-project\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Then, enter the project directory to install 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/max/packages/#whats-included"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 modular}}, which also installs 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 mojo
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ):\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 cd\cf16  example-project\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi \cf14 add\cf16  mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Check the installed version:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi run mojo \cf17 --version\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Manage packages\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Every Pixi project defines its own package dependencies in the local {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/pixi_manifest/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi.toml}} file. When you run {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/add/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi add}} from the project directory, it adds that package name to your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 pixi.toml
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  dependencies.\
You can optionally specify the version with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/version-specifiers/#id5"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 version specifier}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi \cf14 add\cf16  \cf20 "mojo~=0.26.1"\cf16  \cf20 "numpy<2.0"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 If you always want the latest version, you can use the wildcard specifier:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi \cf14 add\cf16  \cf20 "mojo=*"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To update a package, use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/update/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi update}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi update mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This updates the package if there's a new version that adheres to the version you defined (via 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 pixi add
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and as shown in the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 pixi.toml
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  file).\
To remove a package, use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/remove/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi remove}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi remove mojo\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 For more about defining dependencies, read about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/project_configuration/#the-dependencies-tables"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Pixi dependency tables}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Specify the Python version\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Even the Python version is controlled like a package with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/version-specifiers/#id5"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 version specifier}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi \cf14 add\cf16  \cf20 "python==3.11"\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi run python \cf17 --version\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 Python 3.11.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Execute code in the environment\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When your working directory is in a Pixi project (a directory with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/pixi_manifest/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi.toml}} file), you can run any command inside the environment using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/run/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi run}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi run mojo \cf17 --version\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Or, you can open a shell in the environment to run your commands:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi shell\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 mojo \cf17 --version\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Be sure to exit the shell when you're done:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb15 exit\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Clean up environments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can remove Pixi environments using {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/dev/workspace/environment/#cleaning-up"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi clean}}:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi clean\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This removes everything in the Pixi environment, including cached data and built packages. This is particularly useful when iterating on graph builds, as it will clear the MEF cache from the graph compiler.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb23 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb13 pixi clean
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23  removes more than just the MEF cache\'97it removes the entire Pixi environment. You'll need to reinstall packages after running this command.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 The 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb21 pixi.lock
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  file\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Although the project manifest file ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/pixi_manifest/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi.toml}}) defines your project dependencies, it doesn't define the transitive dependencies (the dependencies of your dependencies). Nor does it always specify the exact version that is installed (such as when you {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/version-specifiers/#id5"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 specify a version}} as less-than 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 <
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or greater-than 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb21 >
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
The transitive dependencies and the actually-installed versions are specified in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/workspace/lockfile/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb21 \ul \ulc5 pixi.lock}} file, which is automatically generated\'97you should not edit this file by hand. This file is crucial to ensure that you can reliably reproduce your environment across different machines.\
You can learn more about it from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/features/lockfile/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Pixi lock file docs}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 More reading\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 There's a whole lot more you can do with Pixi, such as create multi-step {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/workspace/advanced_tasks/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 tasks}}, install {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/global_tools/introduction/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 global tools}}, define {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/workspace/multi_environment/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 multiple environments}}, and more.\
For more information, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 official Pixi docs}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pixi.sh/latest/reference/cli/pixi/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 pixi CLI reference}}, or print the help:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf16 \cb15 pixi \cf17 -h\cf16 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

